-{"id": "TJ-DF-037", "type": "task", "priority": "P1", "status": "pending", "title": "[P1] Kernel-matrix teardown verification: prove --kill-child orphan containment per backend per kernel (unshare leaks on Debian 13 / 6.12; bwrap holds on the same box) before the release gate re-opens", "description": "Filed 2026-09-27 from the hands-on review of terminal-jail/terminal-jail @ 6f614783 (v1.2.0), which re-measured the containment claim live on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31 and reconciled it against the Debian 13 evidence. Measured split: on the dev kernel the unshare backend DOES contain (jail PID-ns inode 4026536818 vs host 4026531836, 5 visible PIDs vs 1129) and plugin/test_backend_parity.py::test_live_unshare_orphan_teardown passes in ~0.45s; on fresh Debian 13.7 / kernel 6.12.107 the same test FAILED 3/3 with the payload surviving the wrapper's death for >15s, while the bwrap twin passed on the SAME box in 0.31s. This row is the TESTING that has to take place to resolve it: TJ-GAP-056 (bwrap-vs-unshare containment parity battery) is already status=complete and flagged STALE, so the battery exists and its verdict is dev-kernel-only — the missing artefact is the KERNEL MATRIX that turns one dev-host green into a per-kernel contract. Parent: TJ-DF-027 (failed, P1). Companion: TJ-DF-028 (fresh-machine CI). Gate: RELEASE-TJ-002 (P0 hold).", "acceptance_criteria": "1) One deterministic, committed harness that on ANY host runs the orphan-teardown assertion for BOTH backends (unshare AND bwrap) and prints per cell: kernel string (uname -r), backend, verdict, measured post-death survival seconds, exit code — no dev-host shortcuts, runnable from the documented clone (uv sync --dev) on a bare box. 2) Matrix executed and recorded on >=3 kernels: (a) Ubuntu 26.04 / 7.0.0-31 (dev host), (b) Debian 13.7 / 6.12.107 (fresh bunker agent), (c) one older or newer stable (e.g. 6.8 or 6.14) — every cell with its exact uname -r and raw output quoted in this row. 3) Root cause NAMED, not merely observed: which kernel behaviour differs (candidate: --kill-child propagation vs pid-namespace teardown ordering / PDEATHSIG semantics on 6.12) with a citation (kernel commit, manpage, or a minimal reproducer that demonstrates the mechanism). 4) Contract settled and shipped: EITHER the unshare path tears down on every matrix cell, OR the guarantee is narrowed explicitly (README + --help + docs/backend-parity.md) AND backend selection refuses to pick a leaking backend — auto must not silently select a backend whose teardown contract cannot hold on that kernel. 5) Regression pinned so 'passes on the dev host' stops being the top of the evidence ladder: a kernel-matrix CI cell (or a documented manual gate using the harness) plus the QA battery's fresh-machine ci-pass cell green — closes TJ-DF-028. 6) RELEASE-TJ-002 re-evaluated with the matrix attached; TJ-DF-027 transitions failed->complete or is explicitly re-scoped.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:50:30-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Evidence pointers: TJ-DF-027 (3/3 FAIL, 15.30s each, Debian 13.7 / 6.12.107; bwrap twin PASS 0.31s on the same box); review transcript 2026-09-27 on kernel 7.0.0-31 (bare-mode ns=pid:[4026536818] pids=5; --user ns=pid:[4026533355] HOME=/nonexistent USER=nobody; bwrap /proc/1=bwrap with 5 PIDs vs 1129 host; Seccomp:2 CapEff:0); TJ-GAP-056 complete+STALE; RELEASE-TJ-002 P0 hold; TJ-DF-028 companion. The per-host classifiers scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py and scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py already exist and should be reused by the harness rather than re-invented.", "attempts": 0}
+{"id": "TJ-DF-037", "type": "task", "priority": "P1", "status": "in_progress", "title": "[P1] Kernel-matrix teardown verification: prove --kill-child orphan containment per backend per kernel (unshare leaks on Debian 13 / 6.12; bwrap holds on the same box) before the release gate re-opens", "description": "Filed 2026-09-27 from the hands-on review of terminal-jail/terminal-jail @ 6f614783 (v1.2.0), which re-measured the containment claim live on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31 and reconciled it against the Debian 13 evidence. Measured split: on the dev kernel the unshare backend DOES contain (jail PID-ns inode 4026536818 vs host 4026531836, 5 visible PIDs vs 1129) and plugin/test_backend_parity.py::test_live_unshare_orphan_teardown passes in ~0.45s; on fresh Debian 13.7 / kernel 6.12.107 the same test FAILED 3/3 with the payload surviving the wrapper's death for >15s, while the bwrap twin passed on the SAME box in 0.31s. This row is the TESTING that has to take place to resolve it: TJ-GAP-056 (bwrap-vs-unshare containment parity battery) is already status=complete and flagged STALE, so the battery exists and its verdict is dev-kernel-only — the missing artefact is the KERNEL MATRIX that turns one dev-host green into a per-kernel contract. Parent: TJ-DF-027 (failed, P1). Companion: TJ-DF-028 (fresh-machine CI). Gate: RELEASE-TJ-002 (P0 hold).", "acceptance_criteria": "1) One deterministic, committed harness that on ANY host runs the orphan-teardown assertion for BOTH backends (unshare AND bwrap) and prints per cell: kernel string (uname -r), backend, verdict, measured post-death survival seconds, exit code — no dev-host shortcuts, runnable from the documented clone (uv sync --dev) on a bare box. 2) Matrix executed and recorded on >=3 kernels: (a) Ubuntu 26.04 / 7.0.0-31 (dev host), (b) Debian 13.7 / 6.12.107 (fresh bunker agent), (c) one older or newer stable (e.g. 6.8 or 6.14) — every cell with its exact uname -r and raw output quoted in this row. 3) Root cause NAMED, not merely observed: which kernel behaviour differs (candidate: --kill-child propagation vs pid-namespace teardown ordering / PDEATHSIG semantics on 6.12) with a citation (kernel commit, manpage, or a minimal reproducer that demonstrates the mechanism). 4) Contract settled and shipped: EITHER the unshare path tears down on every matrix cell, OR the guarantee is narrowed explicitly (README + --help + docs/backend-parity.md) AND backend selection refuses to pick a leaking backend — auto must not silently select a backend whose teardown contract cannot hold on that kernel. 5) Regression pinned so 'passes on the dev host' stops being the top of the evidence ladder: a kernel-matrix CI cell (or a documented manual gate using the harness) plus the QA battery's fresh-machine ci-pass cell green — closes TJ-DF-028. 6) RELEASE-TJ-002 re-evaluated with the matrix attached; TJ-DF-027 transitions failed->complete or is explicitly re-scoped.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:50:30-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Evidence pointers: TJ-DF-027 (3/3 FAIL, 15.30s each, Debian 13.7 / 6.12.107; bwrap twin PASS 0.31s on the same box); review transcript 2026-09-27 on kernel 7.0.0-31 (bare-mode ns=pid:[4026536818] pids=5; --user ns=pid:[4026533355] HOME=/nonexistent USER=nobody; bwrap /proc/1=bwrap with 5 PIDs vs 1129 host; Seccomp:2 CapEff:0); TJ-GAP-056 complete+STALE; RELEASE-TJ-002 P0 hold; TJ-DF-028 companion. The per-host classifiers scripts/pidns-capability-probe.py and scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py already exist and should be reused by the harness rather than re-invented.", "attempts": 0, "worker_status": "running", "foreman_note": "Tick terminal-jail-2026-09-27-21-49-22: dispatched kernel-matrix teardown harness worker; no external kernel claims permitted."}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-082", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Filesystem isolation has no working tier on Ubuntu AppArmor hosts: add Landlock (ABI v8 here) as a uid-mapping-free filesystem tier so containment does not depend on setuid-in-userns", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on Ubuntu 26.04 / kernel 7.0.0-31: the jail's filesystem half is ABSENT by default. `terminal-jail --user sh -c ...` prints 'no filesystem isolation — could not create a uid mapping under the user namespace (this host denies setuid inside unprivileged user namespaces, e.g. Ubuntu AppArmor profile 'unprivileged_userns')' and scripts/fs-isolation-probe.py classifies the host DEGRADED (mapped launch rc=1, 'setgroups failed: Operation not permitted'). Only the PID/identity layers hold, so a jailed process still reads the caller's files. This is the same wall Anthropic's sandbox-runtime documents for Ubuntu 24.04+ (kernel.apparmor_restrict_unprivileged_userns strips capabilities from the namespace), so it is an ecosystem fact — but it is the largest single reduction of the project's containment promise on the distro most users install. Landlock needs no uid mapping and no capability-bearing user namespace: landlock_create_ruleset(VERSION) returns ABI 8 on this kernel, and ABI >= 1 already gives filesystem access control. Precedent: OpenAI's Codex CLI uses Landlock for its Linux sandbox. This also subsumes the report's 'srt-style allow/deny path policy' suggestion — a Landlock ruleset IS a kernel-enforced path policy, which is strictly stronger than enumerating secret-read paths in regexes.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A capability probe reports the Landlock ABI (and what it can enforce) per host, alongside the existing pidns-capability-probe.py and fs-isolation-probe.py. 2) When the host permits Landlock but denies uid mapping, the jail enforces a filesystem tier (at minimum: deny reads of the agent credential surfaces — ~/.ssh, ~/.config/terminal-jail token files, hermes secret env, other users' homes — and deny writes outside the working tree) instead of degrading silently to 'no filesystem isolation'. 3) Where Landlock cannot enforce, behaviour is unchanged and still warns loudly; the tier is reported per host in --help/README (docs row DOC-8 tracks the capability matrix). 4) Tests cover: enforcement active on a permitting host (prove a denied read returns EACCES), graceful absence on a non-permitting host, and no regression to the existing unshare/bwrap paths. 5) The per-host report is machine-readable so the fleet can record which tier each image actually gets.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-083", "type": "task", "priority": "P2", "status": "pending", "title": "[P2] Network egress is still evaluable only by regex: add Landlock TCP port rules (ABI >= 4) as a kernel-side egress allowlist behind the rule class", "description": "TJ-GAP-058 closed the network-egress rule CLASS with regexes (/dev/tcp, nc -e, socat EXEC, curl -T, wget --post-file) and it is complete; the review's 22-payload matrix confirms three of those block with quotable rule ids. But regex matching has to out-guess every client, and the review's own honest-gaps list still names 'network egress class' as the largest hole. Landlock ABI >= 4 adds per-port TCP bind/connect control enforced by the kernel, which turns the allowlist from a pattern problem into a policy problem: deny connect except an enumerated port set, regardless of which binary or encoding is used. ABI 8 is available on this kernel. This complements, never replaces, the regex class (regex catches intent and gives a human-readable reason; Landlock catches the syscall).", "acceptance_criteria": "1) With the egress tier enabled, a payload that opens a TCP connection to a non-allowlisted host/port is denied by the KERNEL even when no regex matches it — demonstrated with a case that defeats the current patterns (e.g. a raw python socket or a protocol tunnel). 2) An allowlist exists for legitimate traffic (git push over ssh/https, package registries) and is documented; the deny is fail-closed, the allow is explicit. 3) Verdict/reason for a Landlock denial is distinguishable from a rule-engine denial, so an operator can tell 'a rule said no' from 'the kernel said no'. 4) Degrades honestly on hosts without Landlock ABI >= 4: no silent no-op — either the tier reports unavailable or the request to enable it fails closed. 5) The existing regex class keeps its tests; the new tier's tests prove kernel enforcement, not pattern matching.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
 {"id": "TJ-GAP-084", "type": "task", "priority": "P3", "status": "pending", "title": "[P3] Per-command cost is 65 ms because of Python startup, not judgement: add an optional resident bridge and a documented per-command budget", "description": "Measured 2026-09-27 on the fresh clone: in-process intercept() is p50 2.19-2.24 ms (n=200) and sustains 360 verdicts/s, but the consumer path a host actually pays — the JSON bridge as a subprocess — is p50 65.5 ms / mean 71.2 ms / max 137.6 ms (n=50), dominated by interpreter startup. The firewall is therefore effectively free as a library and ~30x more expensive as the documented integration surface, which matters for a host that fires one verdict per tool call. Fix: a small resident process over a Unix socket in front of the same engine, keeping the one-shot stdin path as the fallback.", "acceptance_criteria": "1) A resident mode exists (documented, optional, no new hard dependency) serving the SAME verdict contract as the stdin bridge; a client that cannot reach it falls back to the one-shot path rather than failing open silently. 2) Measured before/after on the same host with the same payloads: bridge round-trip p50 reported, target well under 10 ms, methodology stated (sample size + host). 3) The daemon is not a new attack surface: no TCP listener, socket permissions documented, and the 'no network surface' property (currently 0 grep hits for any listener) is preserved. 4) A regression guard pins the one-shot path's cost so it cannot silently degrade (see TJ-GAP-086). 5) Docs state which integration the reader should choose and why, with the measured numbers.", "created_at": "2026-09-27T10:53:48-05:00", "created_by": "carter review 2026-09-27 (hands-on, commit 6f614783)", "detail": "Source: hands-on review 2026-09-27, report /home/kara/terminal-jail-carter-review-2026-09-27.html at commit 6f614783.", "attempts": 0}
